"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"has_pull_requests":true,"pull_request_creation_policy":"all","topics":["airflow","apache","apache-airflow","automation","dag","data-engineering","data-integration","data-orchestrator","data-pipelines","data-science","elt","etl","machine-learning","mlops","orchestration","python","scheduler","workflow","workflow-engine","workflow-orchestration"],"visibility":"public","forks":18214,"open_issues":1927,"watchers":47308,"default_branch":"main","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/apache/airflow/pulls/70000","html_url":"https://github.com/apache/airflow/pull/70000","diff_url":"https://github.com/apache/airflow/pull/70000.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/apache/airflow/pull/70000.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"An HTTP connection's `extra` field is documented as a place to put credentials (\"Login and Password authentication can be used along with any authentication method using headers\"), and those end up in `session.headers`, while `run_and_check` follows redirects by default. `requests` strips only `Authorization` once `should_strip_auth` sees the host change, so a secret under any other name (`X-API-Key`, `Private-Token`) is replayed to the redirect target — whether that is an attacker's host reached through an open redirect on the API, or simply the CDN the API bounces downloads to. This gives the connection's own headers the same treatment as `Authorization`; same-host redirects still carry them.\n\n`HttpAsyncHook` has the same gap and `aiohttp` behaves identically, but it offers no equivalent redirect hook, so covering it means reworking the async redirect handling rather than extending an existing one. I left it out to keep this reviewable, and I'm happy to follow up if you'd like it in scope.\n\nThe `FakeSession` patch targets in the sensor tests move to the new class because the hook now instantiates it.\n\n---\n\n####